  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Pseudanos winterbottomi becomes about 15 cm long, so it belongs to the medium-sized headstanders. It should be kept at 26-28°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>This rare headstander was scientifically described only in 2005. It is only very occasionally imported as a by-catch from Venezuela. Mostly the animals were found among Anostomus ternetzi or Pseudanos trimaculatus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Known from the Orinoco in Venezuela and the Tapajós in Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Like all other headstanders, it should be kept in large aquariums rich in cover and planted as well as possible.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>The chemical composition of the water is insignificant; the animals should be cared for at 26-28°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>When feeding, pay attention to the need for vegetable food, otherwise aquarium plants will be eaten. P. winterbottomi eats all common ornamental fish food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>For headstander conditions, the species should be classified as peaceful. Both conspecifics and other fish are usually left alone. The best company are other headstanders, e.g. the species mentioned above, but also other peaceful tetras, cichlids and catfish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pseudanos-winterbottomi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Young animals are brightly ochre colored with a dark vertical stripe, which remains lifelong but is not clearly recognizable in all mood colorings. A golden shine becomes visible with frontlight. With age the fish become darker overall, the caudal fin turns deep orange, and the golden shine is present above all on the back scales. The colour change ability is amazing. The colour scheme is reminiscent of delicacies and fruit offered at Christmas, hence the proposed common name \"Chocolate Headstander\".</p><p>Pseudanos means \"false Anostomus\". winterbottomi is a dedication name for Richard Winterbottom, curator emeritus for ichthyology at the Royal Ontario Museum, Canada.</p>"
    }
  ],
